\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Large language model (LLM) evaluation increasingly relies on standardized benchmarks to compare models and track progress, yet mounting evidence reveals that these benchmarks are systematically compromised by training data contamination \citep{chang2025benchmarking}. \citet{yang2026rl} demonstrate that rephrased benchmark items appearing in training corpora inflate MMLU accuracy by 8--15 percentage points while evading standard $n$-gram decontamination, and that reinforcement learning (RL) post-training with GRPO further amplifies contamination-driven gains while simultaneously concealing the detection signals that would reveal them. Dose-response analyses confirm a monotonic relationship between contamination exposure and score inflation, with effects persisting across model scales \citep{dong2026dose}. Even minor perturbations to benchmark item surface forms---which should not affect genuinely capable models---produce substantial accuracy drops, questioning the validity of reported scores \citep{wang2025fragility}. Together, these findings point to a reliability crisis in LLM evaluation: reported benchmark scores increasingly conflate memorization with generalizable capability \citep{rao2026silicon}.

Several methods have been developed to detect benchmark contamination. Min-K\%++ \citep{duan2025minkpp} and Time Travel \citep{oren2024proving} perform membership inference against pre-training corpora; ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat} measures performance gaps between original and paraphrased items; Self-Critique \citep{wust2025selfcritique} identifies memorization through entropy collapse in model self-evaluations; and KDS \citep{xu2025kds} uses knowledge distillation signatures to flag leaked items. While these methods can detect contamination, none produces calibrated benchmark scores that answer the central evaluation question: \emph{what would this model's accuracy be had it never seen the test data?} On the psychometric side, item response theory (IRT) has been increasingly applied to LLM evaluation. PSN-IRT \citep{gu2026psnirt} models item difficulty and discrimination but does not account for contamination. \citet{polo2026growing} validate IRT at the scale of 400+ models yet treat all items as equally trustworthy. ATLAS \citep{lyu2025atlas} and \citet{lalor2024irt} further develop IRT-based evaluation without contamination modeling, and TinyBenchmarks \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} demonstrates that carefully selected item subsets can approximate full benchmark scores, but again without contamination awareness. HiBayES \citep{zhuang2025hibayes} provides hierarchical Bayesian uncertainty quantification but ignores item compromise. Inference-time decontamination \citep{yang2024inference} and targeted probing \citep{han2026probing} attempt to adjust or detect contamination effects at evaluation time, but neither produces fully calibrated scores with uncertainty quantification. The closest predecessor, DCR \citep{li2025dcr}, provides adjusted accuracy through fuzzy inference over detection signals but offers no uncertainty bounds and does not address the systematic signal degradation under RL post-training that we document in this work.

The case for post-hoc calibration rests on a sobering empirical finding: contamination prevention does not work. \citet{xu2025emperor} systematically evaluate twenty mitigation strategies---including item rephrasing, option shuffling, filtering, and dynamic benchmark construction---and find that none reliably eliminates contamination effects across models and benchmarks. \citet{zhang2025forget} further demonstrate that standard decontamination procedures fail to fully remove memorized content from model weights. While dynamic benchmarks such as LiveBench \citep{white2024livebench} sidestep contamination by continuously generating new items, and efficient evaluation via item subsampling \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} can mitigate item-specific biases, neither approach provides calibrated scores for existing contaminated benchmarks. These findings motivate a paradigm shift from contamination \emph{elimination} to contamination \emph{adjustment}: rather than attempting to remove a confounder that resists removal, we measure it and statistically control for it. The analogy to observational causal inference is instructive---when randomization is infeasible, valid estimates are obtained not by eliminating confounders but by measuring and adjusting for them under clearly stated assumptions. ContamCal instantiates this ``statistical safety net'' philosophy for benchmark evaluation.

Our approach is grounded in educational measurement's longstanding treatment of item compromise---the leaking of test content to examinees prior to administration. Item compromise IRT models \citep{eckerly2017,sinharay2017} incorporate exposure indicators as covariates within the item response function, enabling joint estimation of compromise effects alongside standard item and person parameters. Explanatory IRT \citep{deboeck2004,adams1997} provides the broader theoretical framework for embedding external covariates in item response models. Adapting this psychometric tradition to LLM evaluation requires three non-trivial structural modifications. First, whereas educational IRT assumes independent examinees, LLM evaluation involves individual models producing all responses to a shared item pool, fundamentally altering local independence semantics \citep{mislevy1986}. Second, binary leakage indicators (compromised vs.\ uncompromised) must be replaced with continuous exposure scores derived from noisy, imperfect detection signals---necessitating a measurement error model that propagates detection uncertainty into ability estimates. Third, stable examinee ability must give way to regime-conditional ability, as models undergoing supervised fine-tuning (SFT) versus RL post-training exhibit qualitatively different contamination signatures that affect both detection signal reliability and contamination effect magnitude.
